# Line endings are a property of the repository, not of the machine that cloned
# it. Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf = true` in its *system* gitconfig, so
# without these a Windows checkout rewrites files on the way in and the working
# tree stops being byte-identical to what is committed.
#
# Two things break when that happens, and neither is obvious from the symptom:
#
#   - `gofmt` emits LF and compares byte for byte, so one CRLF source file
#     reports as unformatted on every line - and a whole tree can light up red
#     with nobody having misformatted anything.
#
#   - Any test that hashes a file compares against a value recorded on a Linux
#     machine. Eighteen carriage returns in one SVG are enough to change a
#     SHA-256, which reads as a corrupted asset rather than as a checkout.
#
# `*.svg` is on this list deliberately: it is the extension people forget,
# because it does not look like source.

* text=auto

*.go       text eol=lf
*.mod      text eol=lf
*.sum      text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.js       text eol=lf
*.jsx      text eol=lf
*.ts       text eol=lf
*.tsx      text eol=lf
*.css      text eol=lf
*.html     text eol=lf
*.svg      text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.nsi      text eol=lf

*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.ico      binary
*.icns     binary
*.woff     binary
*.woff2    binary
